<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-solving-trigonometric-equations-i" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Solving Trigonometric Equations I</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurement and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.5 Trigonometry II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Solve trigonometric equations involving trigonometric ratios across the range 0 to 360 degrees
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    12 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To discover, by drawing horizontal lines on the unit circle, that most trig equations have exactly two solutions in <m>[0°, 360°]</m>, and to build geometric intuition for where those solutions lie.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Open with the question: <q>If <m>\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2}</m>, how many values of <m>\theta</m> in <m>[0°, 360°]</m> are possible — just one, or could there be more?</q> Let learners discuss and guess before the activity.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-how-many-angles">
        <title>How Many Angles Give the Same Sine?</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that for a point on the unit circle at angle <m>\theta</m>, the <m>y</m>-coordinate equals <m>\sin\theta</m>. Use this idea to answer the questions below on a unit circle diagram.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Draw the unit circle and the horizontal line <m>y = \frac{1}{2}</m>. How many times does the line cross the circle? Mark both crossing points and estimate the angle <m>\theta</m> at each.
                        </p>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Two crossings: one in Q1 near <m>30°</m>, one in Q2 near <m>150°</m>. Both points have <m>y</m>-coordinate <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, confirming <m>\sin 30° = \sin 150° = \frac{1}{2}</m>. Reinforce that the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the unit circle point equals the sine of the angle.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now draw the horizontal line <m>y = -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. Again mark both crossing points. In which quadrants do they lie? What are the two exact angles?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Both crossings are below the <m>x</m>-axis: one in Q3 at <m>240°</m> and one in Q4 at <m>300°</m>. The reference angle is <m>60°</m> in each case. Connect to CAST: sine is negative in Q3 and Q4.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now try: (i) the line <m>y = 1</m>, and (ii) the line <m>y = 2</m>. How many crossing points does each line have? What does this tell you about the number of solutions to <m>\sin\theta = 1</m> and <m>\sin\theta = 2</m>?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>y = 1</m> is tangent to the circle at exactly one point (<m>\theta = 90°</m>) — one solution. <m>y = 2</m> misses the circle entirely — no solutions. Use this to motivate that <m>\sin\theta \in [-1, 1]</m> for all <m>\theta</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- TODO: Add a JSXGraph interactive here — a horizontal line whose height the learner
         can drag up and down, showing the two intersection points with the unit circle and
         their angles updating in real time. Include the special cases at y = ±1 and the
         "no solution" region outside [-1, 1]. -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the geometric approach into an algebraic method: find the reference angle, then use CAST to identify the two quadrants and compute both solutions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Ask learners to connect the visual approach with the reference angle method from the previous section. Key question: <q>You found both crossings graphically — which quadrants were they in, and how does the CAST rule predict that?</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <p>
        A <term>trigonometric equation</term> asks: for which angle(s) <m>\theta</m> does a given trig ratio equal a particular value? For example, <m>\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2}</m> asks for all angles in <m>[0°, 360°]</m> whose sine is <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        On the unit circle, <m>\sin\theta</m> is the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the point at angle <m>\theta</m>. So solving <m>\sin\theta = k</m> is the same as asking where the horizontal line <m>y = k</m> meets the unit circle. For most <m>k \in (-1, 1)</m> this line crosses the circle in exactly <em>two</em> points, giving two solutions in <m>[0°, 360°]</m>. The exceptions are:
        <ul>
            <li><p><m>k = \pm 1</m>: the line is tangent to the circle — exactly one solution.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>|k| &gt; 1</m>: the line misses the circle entirely — no solutions.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
        Similarly, <m>\cos\theta = k</m> corresponds to the vertical line <m>x = k</m>. The tangent function is unbounded, so <m>\tan\theta = k</m> always has exactly two solutions in <m>[0°, 360°)</m> for any finite <m>k</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sin-half-unit-circle">
        <caption>The horizontal line <m>y = \tfrac{1}{2}</m> crosses the unit circle at <m>P_1</m> (<m>\theta = 30°</m>) and <m>P_2</m> (<m>\theta = 150°</m>) — two solutions to <m>\sin\theta = \tfrac{1}{2}</m>.</caption>
        <image width="52%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.5}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\angA}{30}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\angB}{150}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pAx}{\rr*cos(\angA)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pAy}{\rr*sin(\angA)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pBx}{\rr*cos(\angB)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.15*\rr}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\halfA}{\angA/2}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\halfB}{\angB/2}
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);
                    \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;] (0,-\axlen) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};
                    \draw[red!70!black, thick, dashed] (-\axlen,\pAy) -- (\axlen,\pAy)
                        node[right, font=\small] {$y=\tfrac{1}{2}$};
                    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pAx,\pAy);
                    \draw[orange!80!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pBx,\pAy);
                    \draw[blue!70!black] (0.5,0) arc (0:\angA:0.5);
                    \node[blue!70!black, font=\footnotesize] at (\halfA:0.78) {$30°$};
                    \draw[orange!80!black] (0.7,0) arc (0:\angB:0.7);
                    \node[orange!80!black, font=\footnotesize] at (\halfB:0.98) {$150°$};
                    \filldraw[blue!70!black]   (\pAx,\pAy) circle (2.5pt) node[above right, font=\small] {$P_1$};
                    \filldraw[orange!80!black] (\pBx,\pAy) circle (2.5pt) node[above left,  font=\small] {$P_2$};
                    \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$O$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        <term>Method: reference angle + CAST.</term> To solve any equation of the form <m>\sin\theta = k</m>, <m>\cos\theta = k</m>, or <m>\tan\theta = k</m>:
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find the <term>reference angle</term> <m>\alpha</m>: the acute angle satisfying <m>\sin\alpha = |k|</m> (or <m>\cos\alpha = |k|</m>, or <m>\tan\alpha = |k|</m>).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the CAST rule to identify the two quadrants where the ratio has the correct sign.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Apply the quadrant formula for each solution: Q1 gives <m>\theta = \alpha</m>, Q2 gives <m>\theta = 180° - \alpha</m>, Q3 gives <m>\theta = 180° + \alpha</m>, and Q4 gives <m>\theta = 360° - \alpha</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-trig-equation-quadrants">
        <title>Which quadrants and solutions apply for each equation type</title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                <cell right="minor">Equation</cell>
                <cell right="minor">Sign of <m>k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Quadrants</cell>
                <cell right="minor">Solutions</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\sin\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &gt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q1, Q2</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>\alpha</m> and <m>180°-\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\sin\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &lt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q3, Q4</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>180°+\alpha</m> and <m>360°-\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\cos\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &gt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q1, Q4</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>\alpha</m> and <m>360°-\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\cos\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &lt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q2, Q3</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>180°-\alpha</m> and <m>180°+\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\tan\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &gt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q1, Q3</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>\alpha</m> and <m>180°+\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell right="minor"><m>\tan\theta = k</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>k &lt; 0</m></cell>
                <cell right="minor">Q2, Q4</cell>
                <cell right="minor"><m>180°-\alpha</m> and <m>360°-\alpha</m></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <insight>
        <p>
            To solve <m>\sin\theta = k</m>, <m>\cos\theta = k</m>, or <m>\tan\theta = k</m> for <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m>:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Find the <em>reference angle</em> <m>\alpha</m> (always acute) using <m>|\text{ratio}| = |k|</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use CAST to pick the two correct quadrants and apply the formula:
                        <ul>
                            <li><p><m>\sin\theta = k &gt; 0</m>: Q1 and Q2 → <m>\theta = \alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 180°-\alpha</m></p></li>
                            <li><p><m>\sin\theta = k &lt; 0</m>: Q3 and Q4 → <m>\theta = 180°+\alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 360°-\alpha</m></p></li>
                            <li><p><m>\cos\theta = k &gt; 0</m>: Q1 and Q4 → <m>\theta = \alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 360°-\alpha</m></p></li>
                            <li><p><m>\cos\theta = k &lt; 0</m>: Q2 and Q3 → <m>\theta = 180°-\alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 180°+\alpha</m></p></li>
                            <li><p><m>\tan\theta = k &gt; 0</m>: Q1 and Q3 → <m>\theta = \alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 180°+\alpha</m></p></li>
                            <li><p><m>\tan\theta = k &lt; 0</m>: Q2 and Q4 → <m>\theta = 180°-\alpha</m> or <m>\theta = 360°-\alpha</m></p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>|k| = 1</m> for sine or cosine, there is exactly one solution; if <m>|k| &gt; 1</m>, there are no solutions.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Solving <m>\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2}</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m> satisfying <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The reference angle satisfies <m>\sin\alpha = \dfrac{1}{2}</m> with <m>\alpha</m> acute. Since <m>\sin 30° = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>, we have <m>\alpha = 30°</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>k = \dfrac{1}{2} &gt; 0</m>, the CAST rule places sine positive in Q1 and Q2:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{Q1:} \quad \theta \amp= 30°</mrow>
                    <mrow>\text{Q2:} \quad \theta \amp= 180° - 30° = 150°</mrow>
                </md>
                The solutions are <m>\theta = 30°</m> and <m>\theta = 150°</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Solving <m>\cos\theta = -\frac{1}{2}</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m> satisfying <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The reference angle satisfies <m>\cos\alpha = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>. Since <m>\cos 60° = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>, we have <m>\alpha = 60°</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>k = -\dfrac{1}{2} &lt; 0</m>, the CAST rule places cosine negative in Q2 and Q3:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{Q2:} \quad \theta \amp= 180° - 60° = 120°</mrow>
                    <mrow>\text{Q3:} \quad \theta \amp= 180° + 60° = 240°</mrow>
                </md>
                The solutions are <m>\theta = 120°</m> and <m>\theta = 240°</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Solving <m>\tan\theta = \sqrt{3}</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m> satisfying <m>\tan\theta = \sqrt{3}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The reference angle satisfies <m>\tan\alpha = \sqrt{3}</m>. Since <m>\tan 60° = \sqrt{3}</m>, we have <m>\alpha = 60°</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>k = \sqrt{3} &gt; 0</m>, the CAST rule places tangent positive in Q1 and Q3:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{Q1:} \quad \theta \amp= 60°</mrow>
                    <mrow>\text{Q3:} \quad \theta \amp= 180° + 60° = 240°</mrow>
                </md>
                The solutions are <m>\theta = 60°</m> and <m>\theta = 240°</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Solving a Decimal Equation</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m> satisfying <m>\sin\theta = -0.6</m>. Give answers correct to 1 decimal place.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The reference angle satisfies <m>\sin\alpha = 0.6</m>, so <m>\alpha = \sin^{-1}(0.6) \approx 36.9°</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>k = -0.6 &lt; 0</m>, the CAST rule places sine negative in Q3 and Q4:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{Q3:} \quad \theta \amp= 180° + 36.9° = 216.9°</mrow>
                    <mrow>\text{Q4:} \quad \theta \amp= 360° - 36.9° = 323.1°</mrow>
                </md>
                The solutions are <m>\theta \approx 216.9°</m> and <m>\theta \approx 323.1°</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-exact">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve each equation for <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m>, giving exact answers.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{1}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = 1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = -1</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>60°</m> and <m>120°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>60°</m> and <m>300°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>45°</m> and <m>225°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>210°</m> and <m>330°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>150°</m> and <m>210°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>135°</m> and <m>315°</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-decimal">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve each equation for <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m>, giving answers correct to 1 decimal place.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = 0.8</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = 0.6</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = 2</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = -0.4</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = -0.3</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>53.1°</m> and <m>126.9°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>53.1°</m> and <m>306.9°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>63.4°</m> and <m>243.4°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>203.6°</m> and <m>336.4°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>107.5°</m> and <m>252.5°</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-special">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve each equation for <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = 1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = -1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>90°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>180°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0°</m> and <m>180°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>90°</m> and <m>270°</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-count">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each equation, state the number of solutions in <m>[0°, 360°]</m> without solving. Explain your reasoning.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = 0.7</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = -1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = 5</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = 2</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = 0</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>2 — sine positive, two quadrants (Q1 and Q2)</p></li>
                        <li><p>1 — boundary case <m>k = -1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>2 — tangent always has two solutions per full turn</p></li>
                        <li><p>0 — impossible since <m>|k| &gt; 1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>2 — <m>\theta = 90°</m> and <m>270°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>2 — <m>\theta = 0°</m> and <m>180°</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-unit-circle-point">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A point <m>P</m> lies on the unit circle and has <m>y</m>-coordinate <m>\dfrac{3}{5}</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find all possible values of <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m>. Give your answers correct to 1 decimal place.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the exact <m>x</m>-coordinates of the corresponding points.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{3}{5} = 0.6</m>, so the reference angle is <m>\alpha = \sin^{-1}(0.6) \approx 36.9°</m>. Since <m>k &gt; 0</m>: Q1 gives <m>\theta \approx 36.9°</m> and Q2 gives <m>\theta \approx 143.1°</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                From <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>: <m>x = \pm\sqrt{1 - \dfrac{9}{25}} = \pm\dfrac{4}{5}</m>. The Q1 point is <m>\left(\dfrac{4}{5},\, \dfrac{3}{5}\right)</m> and the Q2 point is <m>\left(-\dfrac{4}{5},\, \dfrac{3}{5}\right)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="trig-equations-i-applied">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The horizontal displacement of a point on a rotating arm is modelled by <m>d = \cos\theta</m>, where <m>\theta</m> is the angle of rotation from the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Find all angles <m>\theta \in [0°, 360°]</m> for which <m>d = -0.5</m>, and explain what each solution means in context.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Solving <m>\cos\theta = -0.5 = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>: the reference angle is <m>\alpha = 60°</m>, and cosine is negative in Q2 and Q3:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\theta \amp= 180° - 60° = 120°</mrow>
                        <mrow>\theta \amp= 180° + 60° = 240°</mrow>
                    </md>
                    At <m>\theta = 120°</m> the arm has rotated anticlockwise into the upper-left region; at <m>\theta = 240°</m> it is in the lower-left region. Both positions give the same horizontal displacement of <m>-0.5</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

